a number called gen_bufs is 0.

(Scratch views/accumulators, program level and 'clear'ed per call
 (Defect 17): a fresh declaration per call is never freed until program
 exit, so a per-call buffer is one 4 KB allocation per call, unbounded
 over a campaign - regardless of how few bytes it actually holds.)

a buffer called 'the one-word check view' is "".

a buffer called 'the buffer reference view' is "".

a buffer called 'the buffer filler bytes' is "".

a buffer called 'the mutated content view' is "".

(Buffers: declaration, a write, and a read - the language's own claim
 is that out-of-bounds access sets the error flag and returns 0 rather
 than crashing, so the oob variant is what actually puts that claim on
 trial.)

To 'gen leaf buffer inrange' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_bufs to gen_bufs add 1.
    a number called 'buf size' is 'rng below' of 64 add 8.
    a text called decl is "a buffer called b{gen_bufs} is {'buf size'} bytes in size".
    a number called pos is 'rng below' of 'buf size' add 1.
    a number called val is 'rng below' of 256.
    a text called 'write line' is "Set byte {pos} of b{gen_bufs} to {val}".
    a text called 'read line' is "Print byte {pos} of b{gen_bufs}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'write line', 'read line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen leaf buffer oob' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_bufs to gen_bufs add 1.
    a number called 'buf size' is 'rng below' of 64 add 8.
    a text called decl is "a buffer called b{gen_bufs} is {'buf size'} bytes in size".
    a number called extra is 'rng below' of 500 add 1.
    a number called oob is 'buf size' add extra.
    a text called 'read line' is "Print byte {oob} of b{gen_bufs}".
    a text called handler is "On error print \"buffer read out of range\"".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'read line', handler].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(--- The ledger's buffer leaves, batch A ------------------------------

 docs/ledger/buffers.md maps LANGUAGE.md:3515-3801 into numbered claims;
 everything below puts one of those claims on trial and ASSERTS its
 documented result, so a wrong answer is a finding rather than a line of
 output nobody reads. A failing assertion prints

     ASSERT BUF-05: expected 4097 got 4096

 and exits 95, which loop_gen.vox classifies as a wrong-value finding
 carrying that line as its detail. Every claim below was run against the
 real compiler before it was encoded - the probes are in
 docs/ledger/probes/buffers/ and the batch's own report lists what each
 one printed.

 Rows covered here: BUF-01, BUF-03, BUF-05, BUF-07, BUF-10, BUF-11,
 BUF-12, BUF-13.)

(Names. CLAUDE.md's rule is that any sameness across generated programs
 needs a citation from the manual, and `b1`, `b2` have none - they are a
 habit, and a habit that puts the same identifier in 100% of the corpus.
 These are the words the generated programs' buffers are actually called.
 Twenty-nine of them, mostly things you would really put bytes into, so
 a program reads as English and the invariant report has nothing to flag.

 A word is claimed at most once per program - a redeclaration would not
 compile - and only when a program runs the list out does a numeric
 suffix appear at all.

 There were thirty. `the parcel` was removed on 2026-08-21: it was also
 in 'the value nouns' (gen_collections.vox), and both pools declare
 TOP-LEVEL names, which share one global identifier space
 (LANGUAGE.md:1688, "Type names, variable names, and function names
 share a single global identifier namespace"). A seed that drew it for a
 buffer and for a `value` in the same program wrote the name twice and
 did not compile - a rare, seed-dependent failure that looked like
 nothing in particular. It came out of THIS pool rather than out of the
 value nouns because the value nouns are spent on a cycle whose stride
 must be coprime with the list's length, so that list has to stay a
 prime number of words long (41) and this one does not: 'gen buffer
 name' walks claimed-word marks, so twenty-nine is as good as thirty.
 tests/230_units.vox counts the overlap and it must read 0.)

a list called gen_buffer_words is ["the inbox", "scratch", "the greeting", "ledger", "outgoing post", "the pigeonhole", "satchel", "the mailbag", "workbench", "the sketchpad", "the postbag", "notebook", "the envelope", "the tray", "cupboard", "the dispatch note", "the register", "pantry", "the manifest", "the shipping label", "toolbox", "the drawer", "the waybill", "the message slip", "loading bay", "the pallet", "the crate", "storeroom", "the tally sheet"].

a list called gen_buffer_word_taken is [].

a number called gen_buffer_names is 0.

(The unquoted word behind the name 'gen buffer name' just handed out.
 A leaf that needs COMPANION names for the same buffer - a loop counter,
 an error-flag boolean - builds them from this, so they read as English
 too and stay unique for the same reason the buffer's own name does.)

a text called gen_buffer_last_word is "".

(Out-parameters for 'gen buffer known filler'/'gen buffer known filler
 mutated' (batch C, below): the ascii value of whichever position each
 call was asked to remember, since a Vox function returns one value and
 the payload text is the one those two callers actually want back.)

a number called gen_buffer_marked_value is 0.

a number called gen_buffer_second_marked_value is 0.

(Called once per program from 'gen program', beside the other counter
 resets: the claimed-word marks are per program, not per campaign.)

To 'gen buffer names reset'.
    Set gen_buffer_names to 0.
    Set gen_buffer_word_taken to [].
    a number called 'how many words' is gen_buffer_words's length.
    a number called 'the word position' is 0.
    While 'the word position' is less than 'how many words',
        append 0 to gen_buffer_word_taken,
        Set 'the word position' to 'the word position' add 1.

(Does this name have to be quoted where it is written? LANGUAGE.md:760
 requires the single quotes when the name contains a space; as of 0.4.15
 (#110) a single word may also be quoted, but need not be.)

To 'gen name is one word' with a text called 'the name'.
    clear 'the one-word check view'.
    append 'the name' to 'the one-word check view'.
    a number called 'the byte count' is 'the one-word check view's size.
    a number called 'the position' is 1.
    a boolean called 'a space was seen' is false.
    While 'the position' is less than or equal to 'the byte count',
        a number called 'the byte here' is byte 'the position' of 'the one-word check view',
        If 'the byte here' is 32 then,
            Set 'a space was seen' to true.
        Set 'the position' to 'the position' add 1.
    If 'a space was seen' then,
        Return a boolean, false.
    Return a boolean, true.

(One unused word from the vocabulary, as a possessive STEM rather than
 as the finished name. The scan starts from a random word and walks
 forward, wrapping, so every word is reachable from every draw and no
 word is handed out twice in one program.

 A one-word name is legal bare AND legal single-quoted - hand-verified
 both ways against 0.4.7 - so which spelling appears is not pinned by
 any rule, and is therefore drawn. Through a prime modulus, not `'rng
 below' of 2: this LCG's low bits are correlated, which is documented
 above 'gen leaf loop control' and has caught this generator out before.

 Why a stem: a quoted name's CLOSING QUOTE and the apostrophe of its
 possessive are the same character - `'the inbox's size`, not
 `'the inbox''s size`. Concatenating "'s" onto a finished quoted name
 produces the doubled form, which the compiler does accept, so it
 survives every gate and only shows up as unreadable noise in the
 corpus. Handing back the stem (`'the inbox`, no closing quote) makes
 both spellings fall out of one rule: 'gen buffer reference' adds the
 quote, 'gen buffer property' adds the possessive.)

To 'gen buffer name'.
    Set gen_buffer_names to gen_buffer_names add 1.
    a number called 'how many words' is gen_buffer_words's length.
    a number called 'the draw' is 'rng below' of 'how many words'.
    a number called 'the chosen word number' is 0.
    a number called 'the steps taken' is 0.
    While 'the steps taken' is less than 'how many words',
        a number called 'the offset sum' is 'the draw' add 'the steps taken',
        a number called 'the wrapped' is 'the offset sum' modulo 'how many words',
        a number called 'the candidate' is 'the wrapped' add 1,
        a number called 'the candidate is taken' is element 'the candidate' of gen_buffer_word_taken,
        If 'the candidate is taken' is 0 and 'the chosen word number' is 0 then,
            Set 'the chosen word number' to 'the candidate'.
        Set 'the steps taken' to 'the steps taken' add 1.
    Set gen_buffer_last_word to "".
    If 'the chosen word number' is 0 then,
        (Every word is spoken for. Only a program that declares more than
         thirty buffers gets here, and only then does a name carry a
         number - which is what keeps the suffix out of the corpus's
         ordinary shape.)
        a number called 'the fallback draw' is 'rng below' of 'how many words',
        a number called 'the fallback number' is 'the fallback draw' add 1,
        a text called 'the fallback word' is element 'the fallback number' of gen_buffer_words,
        Set gen_buffer_last_word to "{'the fallback word'} {gen_buffer_names}".
    If 'the chosen word number' is not 0 then,
        Set element 'the chosen word number' of gen_buffer_word_taken to 1,
        a text called 'the free word' is element 'the chosen word number' of gen_buffer_words,
        Set gen_buffer_last_word to 'the free word'.
    a boolean called 'the name is one word' is 'gen name is one word' of gen_buffer_last_word.
    a number called 'the quoting draw' is 'rng below' of 13.
    If 'the name is one word' and 'the quoting draw' is less than 6 then,
        Return a text, gen_buffer_last_word.
    a text called 'the quoted stem' is "'{gen_buffer_last_word}".
    Return a text, 'the quoted stem'.

(The name as it is WRITTEN - the stem, closed with its quote if it
 opened with one.)

To 'gen buffer reference' with a text called 'the stem'.
    clear 'the buffer reference view'.
    append 'the stem' to 'the buffer reference view'.
    a number called 'the first byte' is byte 1 of 'the buffer reference view'.
    If 'the first byte' is 39 then,
        Return a text, "{'the stem'}'".
    Return a text, 'the stem'.

(A property of that buffer - `ledger's size`, `'the inbox's capacity`.
 One apostrophe either way, which is the whole reason the stem exists.)

To 'gen buffer property' with a text called 'the stem' and a text called 'the property'.
    a text called 'the access' is "{'the stem'}'s {'the property'}".
    Return a text, 'the access'.

(A companion name for the buffer just named - a counter, a flag - built
 from the same word so the generated program still reads aloud. Always
 quoted, because it always contains spaces.)

To 'gen buffer companion' with a text called role.
    a text called 'the companion' is "'{gen_buffer_last_word} {role}'".
    Return a text, 'the companion'.

(Capacities. LANGUAGE.md:3345-3347 fixes only that the capacity IS the
 number written; it says nothing about which numbers may be written, so
 a leaf with a favourite size would be inventing a rule. The named
 values are the boundaries worth landing on deliberately - both sides of
 the 4096-byte reserve a dynamic buffer turns out to start with
 (docs/ledger/buffers.md, Discrepancy 1), the one- and two-byte floor,
 and one size well past the reserve - and everything else is a wide
 uniform draw.)

To 'gen buffer size'.
    a number called pick is 'rng below' of 16.
    If pick is 0 then,
        Return a number, 1.
    If pick is 1 then,
        Return a number, 2.
    If pick is 2 then,
        Return a number, 7.
    If pick is 3 then,
        Return a number, 64.
    If pick is 4 then,
        Return a number, 4095.
    If pick is 5 then,
        Return a number, 4096.
    If pick is 6 then,
        Return a number, 4097.
    If pick is 7 then,
        Return a number, 70000.
    a number called 'the wide draw' is 'rng below' of 100000.
    a number called 'the wide size' is 'the wide draw' add 1.
    Return a number, 'the wide size'.

(BUF-01/BUF-05: the three spellings that declare a buffer of a stated
 capacity. `is N bytes in size` is LANGUAGE.md:3345; `Create ... with
 size N` is the worked example at LANGUAGE.md:3543; `is N bytes` is the
 spelling the compiler's own no-size warning recommends, and it compiles
 - hand-verified, all three, capacity exactly N. Nothing says one of
 them is canonical, so all three are drawn.)

To 'gen buffer declaration' with a text called 'the name' and a number called 'the size'.
    a number called 'the spelling' is 'rng below' of 3.
    If 'the spelling' is 0 then,
        Return a text, "a buffer called {'the name'} is {'the size'} bytes in size".
    If 'the spelling' is 1 then,
        Return a text, "a buffer called {'the name'} is {'the size'} bytes".
    Return a text, "Create a buffer called {'the name'} with size {'the size'}".

(BUF-01: the other declaration form, with no size at all. Both spellings
 the manual gives - LANGUAGE.md:3515 and the bare `Create` at
 LANGUAGE.md:537, whose annotation now reads "starts with 4096 bytes of capacity, size 0" (vox #93).
 The compiler prints a no-size warning for either; a warning is not a
 compile failure and the program runs.)

To 'gen dynamic buffer declaration' with a text called 'the name'.
    a number called 'the spelling' is 'rng below' of 13.
    If 'the spelling' is less than 6 then,
        Return a text, "a buffer called {'the name'}".
    Return a text, "Create a buffer called {'the name'}".

(One format slot, `{inner}`, in the generated source. The doubled braces
 are this file's own escape - `{{` emits one `{` - the same idiom
 'gen format slot' uses in gen_text.vox.)

To 'gen buffer slot of' with a text called 'the inner'.
    a text called 'the slot' is "{{{'the inner'}}}".
    Return a text, 'the slot'.

(An assertion, as one renderable line.

 The shape is TWO statements: `If <wrong> then, <report>, Exit 95.` -
 which closes on its own period - followed by a plain `Print <read>`,
 which is what takes whatever punctuation 'gen join lines' adds. Both
 halves are load-bearing:

 - the If must close ITSELF, because a line that left a clause open
   would swallow the comma join uses to continue an enclosing block, and
   every line after it would silently become part of the assertion
   instead of part of the block;
 - the trailing Print must be there, because the join has to have a
   plain statement to punctuate. It also makes the line safe as a
   block's closer (note 6b), and puts the observed value in the corpus
   where the invariant report can watch it vary.

 The obvious shape - ending on `Otherwise, Print <read>` - looks
 equivalent and is not: the comma then continues the OTHERWISE branch,
 so every following statement in the block nests one level deeper than
 intended. It survived a hand-probe (the else branch runs, so the output
 looks right) and was caught by seed 30 of the first campaign, where a
 top-level declaration after the block ended up declared inside it and
 the program stopped compiling. Kept here because the two shapes are
 one comma apart and the wrong one passes a casual test.)

To 'gen assert number' with a text called 'the row' and a text called 'the subject' and a text called 'the expected' and a text called 'the expected shown'.
    a text called 'the got shown' is 'gen buffer slot of' of 'the subject'.
    a text called 'the line' is "If {'the subject'} is not {'the expected'} then, Print \"ASSERT {'the row'}: expected {'the expected shown'} got {'the got shown'}\", Exit 95. Print {'the subject'}".
    Return a text, 'the line'.

(The common case: the generator knows the answer as a literal number, so
 the expected value reads the same in the test and in the message.)

To 'gen assert number is' with a text called 'the row' and a text called 'the subject' and a number called 'the expected'.
    a text called 'the expected text' is "{'the expected'}".
    a text called 'the line' is 'gen assert number' of 'the row' and 'the subject' and 'the expected text' and 'the expected text'.
    Return a text, 'the line'.

(The other case: the expected value is another property of the same
 buffer, so it has to be READ in the generated program rather than baked
 in - the test compares the two properties and the message interpolates
 whichever one the program actually holds.)

To 'gen assert number matches' with a text called 'the row' and a text called 'the subject' and a text called 'the expected expression'.
    a text called 'the expected shown' is 'gen buffer slot of' of 'the expected expression'.
    a text called 'the line' is 'gen assert number' of 'the row' and 'the subject' and 'the expected expression' and 'the expected shown'.
    Return a text, 'the line'.

(Booleans have no `is not` form worth emitting here - the property IS
 the condition, which is the whole point of naming it `empty` or `full` -
 so the two directions are two lines rather than one with a flag.)

To 'gen assert true' with a text called 'the row' and a text called 'the subject'.
    a text called 'the got shown' is 'gen buffer slot of' of 'the subject'.
    a text called 'the line' is "If {'the subject'} is false then, Print \"ASSERT {'the row'}: expected 1 got {'the got shown'}\", Exit 95. Print {'the subject'}".
    Return a text, 'the line'.

To 'gen assert false' with a text called 'the row' and a text called 'the subject'.
    a text called 'the got shown' is 'gen buffer slot of' of 'the subject'.
    a text called 'the line' is "If {'the subject'} then, Print \"ASSERT {'the row'}: expected 0 got {'the got shown'}\", Exit 95. Print {'the subject'}".
    Return a text, 'the line'.

(A payload of exactly this many bytes, so the size it produces is a
 number the generator can assert. Letters and digits only: a brace would
 open a format slot and a quote or a backslash would end the literal,
 and either would make the emitted program mean something other than
 what was counted.)

To 'gen buffer filler' with a number called 'how many bytes'.
    clear 'the buffer filler bytes'.
    a number called 'the written' is 0.
    While 'the written' is less than 'how many bytes',
        a number called 'the pick' is 'rng below' of 36,
        append "{'gen digit for value' of 'the pick'}" to 'the buffer filler bytes',
        Set 'the written' to 'the written' add 1.
    Return a text, 'the buffer filler bytes' as text.

(An unrelated statement, for the gap between a declaration and the use
 of it. LANGUAGE.md nowhere requires a buffer to be used where it is
 declared, so "declaration and use are adjacent" is exactly the kind of
 undeclared rule CLAUDE.md is about; this is what breaks it.)

To 'gen buffer interlude'.
    a text called 'the expression' is 'gen expr'.
    a text called 'the line' is "Print {'the expression'}".
    Return a text, 'the line'.

(BUF-01, BUF-05: the two declaration forms, and the capacity a stated
 size actually buys.

 BUF-01 (LANGUAGE.md:3515-3541) says buffers come in two forms, dynamic
 and fixed. Both are emitted, drawn per buffer, and both are asserted to
 hold nothing yet: LANGUAGE.md:537 annotates the bare `Create` form as
 "0 bytes", and a stated capacity is capacity, not content - the fixed
 form starts empty too, hand-verified for all three sized spellings.
 That assertion sits immediately after the declaration, which is the
 only thing about this leaf's ordering that is pinned.

 BUF-05 (LANGUAGE.md:3345-3347) says the stated size is allocated
 exactly. Asserted against the number the generator wrote, over sizes
 from one byte to seventy thousand - see 'gen buffer size' for why the
 range is not narrower.

 How many buffers, which form each takes, which of the five spellings,
 what the size is, and whether an unrelated statement sits between the
 declaration and the capacity check are all drawn. Nothing in the manual
 pins any of them.)

To 'gen leaf buffer declaration' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a number called 'how many buffers' is 'rng below' of 3 add 1.
    a list called 'the lines' is [].
    a number called 'the buffers made' is 0.
    a text called 'the last name' is "".
    While 'the buffers made' is less than 'how many buffers',
        a text called 'the stem' is 'gen buffer name',
        a text called 'the name' is 'gen buffer reference' of 'the stem',
        a text called 'the size access' is 'gen buffer property' of 'the stem' and "size",
        a number called 'the form' is 'rng below' of 13,
        If 'the form' is less than 6 then,
            a text called 'the dynamic declaration' is 'gen dynamic buffer declaration' of 'the name',
            append 'the dynamic declaration' to 'the lines',
            a text called 'the dynamic emptiness' is 'gen assert number is' of "BUF-01" and 'the size access' and 0,
            append 'the dynamic emptiness' to 'the lines'.
        If 'the form' is greater than 5 then,
            a number called 'the size' is 'gen buffer size',
            a text called 'the fixed declaration' is 'gen buffer declaration' of 'the name' and 'the size',
            append 'the fixed declaration' to 'the lines',
            a text called 'the fixed emptiness' is 'gen assert number is' of "BUF-01" and 'the size access' and 0,
            append 'the fixed emptiness' to 'the lines',
            a number called 'the interlude draw' is 'rng below' of 13,
            If 'the interlude draw' is less than 5 then,
                a text called 'the interlude' is 'gen buffer interlude',
                append 'the interlude' to 'the lines'.
            a text called 'the capacity access' is 'gen buffer property' of 'the stem' and "capacity",
            a text called 'the capacity assertion' is 'gen assert number is' of "BUF-05" and 'the capacity access' and 'the size',
            append 'the capacity assertion' to 'the lines'.
        Set 'the last name' to 'the size access',
        Set 'the buffers made' to 'the buffers made' add 1.
    (A plain read closes the group. An If/Otherwise as a block's last
     statement traps whatever follows it - hand-reduced, and the reason
     'gen leaf rich condition' stays out of the closer pool - so every
     leaf here ends on a statement that closes what it is in.)
    a text called 'the closing read' is "Print {'the last name'}".
    append 'the closing read' to 'the lines'.
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(BUF-10, BUF-11, BUF-12: what the size, length, capacity and empty
 properties report, before and after a write whose length the generator
 chose.

 BUF-10 (LANGUAGE.md:3441, 3445): size is bytes stored, capacity is
 bytes holdable. The payload is built to a known length, so both are
 numbers the generator can assert outright.

 BUF-11 (LANGUAGE.md:3442): length is the same as size. Asserted twice -
 against the literal the generator expects, and against `size` itself,
 because a synonym that agreed with the literal while disagreeing with
 its own twin would be a stranger bug and is worth catching separately.

 BUF-12 (LANGUAGE.md:3609): empty is true exactly while size is 0.
 Asserted before and after the append. Note this is the PROPERTY
 `X's empty`, not the `is empty` predicate 'gen leaf stdin read'
 already emits - different construct, different code path, and the
 property was the one no leaf ever read.

 The payload is capped well under the capacity so nothing truncates
 here; truncation is BUF-06/BUF-07's claim, not this one's.)

To 'gen leaf buffer properties' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called 'the stem' is 'gen buffer name'.
    a text called 'the name' is 'gen buffer reference' of 'the stem'.
    a text called 'the size access' is 'gen buffer property' of 'the stem' and "size".
    a text called 'the length access' is 'gen buffer property' of 'the stem' and "length".
    a text called 'the capacity access' is 'gen buffer property' of 'the stem' and "capacity".
    a text called 'the emptiness access' is 'gen buffer property' of 'the stem' and "empty".
    a number called 'the size' is 'gen buffer size'.
    a number called 'the payload draw' is 'rng below' of 64 add 1.
    a number called 'the payload length' is 'the payload draw'.
    If 'the payload draw' is greater than 'the size' then,
        Set 'the payload length' to 'the size'.
    a text called 'the payload' is 'gen buffer filler' of 'the payload length'.
    a text called 'the declaration' is 'gen buffer declaration' of 'the name' and 'the size'.
    a text called 'the emptiness before' is 'gen assert true' of "BUF-12" and 'the emptiness access'.
    a text called 'the append line' is "append \"{'the payload'}\" to {'the name'}".
    a text called 'the size check' is 'gen assert number is' of "BUF-10" and 'the size access' and 'the payload length'.
    a text called 'the capacity check' is 'gen assert number is' of "BUF-10" and 'the capacity access' and 'the size'.
    a text called 'the length check' is 'gen assert number is' of "BUF-11" and 'the length access' and 'the payload length'.
    a text called 'the synonym check' is 'gen assert number matches' of "BUF-11" and 'the length access' and 'the size access'.
    a text called 'the emptiness after' is 'gen assert false' of "BUF-12" and 'the emptiness access'.
    a text called 'the closing read' is "Print {'the length access'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['the declaration', 'the emptiness before', 'the append line', 'the size check', 'the capacity check', 'the length check', 'the synonym check', 'the emptiness after', 'the closing read'].
    (An unrelated statement between the declaration and the first read,
     sometimes: nothing in the manual makes them adjacent.)
    a number called 'the interlude draw' is 'rng below' of 13.
    If 'the interlude draw' is less than 5 then,
        a text called 'the interlude' is 'gen buffer interlude',
        Set 'the lines' to ['the declaration', 'the emptiness before', 'the interlude', 'the append line', 'the size check', 'the capacity check', 'the length check', 'the synonym check', 'the emptiness after', 'the closing read'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(BUF-13: full is true exactly when size equals capacity.

 LANGUAGE.md:3610 states it "for fixed buffers", so a fixed buffer is
 what gets asserted: the appends are sized to reach the capacity exactly,
 in one to three pieces, and `full` is asserted false after every piece
 but the last and true after it.

 A dynamic buffer sometimes gets the property too, and deliberately
 WITHOUT an assertion. The manual scopes the claim to fixed buffers, so
 there is no documented answer to assert against - a dynamic buffer's
 capacity is its current reserve rather than anything the program asked
 for, and asserting our reading of what `full` should mean there is
 exactly the false-finding factory CLAUDE.md warns about. It is emitted
 because reading the property must not crash, which is a claim of its
 own; what it returns is the ledger's question, not this leaf's.

 The capacity here is small - it has to be reachable by literal appends
 the generated source can carry - which is why BUF-05's wide capacity
 sweep lives in 'gen leaf buffer declaration' instead.)

To 'gen leaf buffer fullness' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called 'the stem' is 'gen buffer name'.
    a text called 'the name' is 'gen buffer reference' of 'the stem'.
    a text called 'the fullness access' is 'gen buffer property' of 'the stem' and "full".
    a text called 'the size access' is 'gen buffer property' of 'the stem' and "size".
    a number called 'the capacity draw' is 'rng below' of 96.
    a number called 'the capacity' is 'the capacity draw' add 1.
    a text called 'the declaration' is 'gen buffer declaration' of 'the name' and 'the capacity'.
    a list called 'the lines' is ['the declaration'].
    a text called 'the fullness before' is 'gen assert false' of "BUF-13" and 'the fullness access'.
    append 'the fullness before' to 'the lines'.
    a number called 'the piece count' is 'rng below' of 3 add 1.
    If 'the piece count' is greater than 'the capacity' then,
        Set 'the piece count' to 'the capacity'.
    a number called 'the bytes left' is 'the capacity'.
