(BUF-03: a dynamic buffer grows without bound.

 LANGUAGE.md:3526 promises no overflow and automatic growth; the
 ledger's own probe found the buffer starts with a 4096-byte reserve
 rather than the zero capacity the manual claims (Discrepancy 1, still
 open), so the interesting question is what happens PAST that reserve.
 The loop appends a piece of known length a number of times the
 generator chose, always to a total well over 4096, and the size is
 asserted against the product. The error flag is checked too: growth
 that silently truncated would leave the size wrong, but growth that
 succeeded while raising the flag would be a different bug, and only the
 On error catches that one.

 The flag is CLEARED first, and that line is load-bearing. Vox's error
 flag stays pending until an On error consumes it (hand-verified: an
 unhandled `10 divide 0` earlier in the program fires the next On error
 anywhere below it, and consuming it there stops it firing again). The
 first version of this leaf asserted the flag without clearing, and seed
 12 of the very first campaign duly reported a BUF-03 failure that was
 really a division by zero forty lines earlier - a false finding wearing
 a ledger row's name, which is precisely the failure mode CLAUDE.md
 warns this work can become. The clearing On error consumes whatever the
 rest of the program left pending, so what the loop's own handler sees
 was raised by the loop.

 Written as ONE line, deliberately. A While rendered across several
 lines would take a period from 'gen join lines' on its first line and
 close with an empty body - the reason 'gen leaf while' is shaped the
 same way. The internal period after the handler closes the handler
 only, so the loop body continues and the joined period closes the loop.
 Hand-verified as emitted, not merely as intended.

 Top level only: it carries a loop and an On error, and both of those
 make it unsafe as the statement that closes an enclosing block.)

