(--- The ledger's buffer leaves, night of 2026-08-28 -------------------

 This batch covers docs/ledger/buffers.md rows BUF-06, BUF-11, BUF-15,
 BUF-16, BUF-17, BUF-29, BUF-30 and BUF-33 (PROCEDURE.md 6: one batch,
 at most eight rows). BUF-11 is not a fresh row: the earlier batch above
 already asserts `X's length is X's size`, but only ever right after an
 append (see 'gen leaf buffer properties'). That is a sameness CLAUDE.md
 would flag once the invariant report saw enough programs - the row's
 own wording lists append, set byte and clear as the mutations to draw
 between, so 'gen leaf buffer length synonym' below adds the missing
 randomness instead of asserting the same append case a second time.

 Every claim here was hand-run against the installed vox v0.4.14 before
 being encoded; the probes and their output are in
 REPORT-LEAVES-BUFFERS-A.md.)

(A text/buffer equality assertion. 'the expected' is embedded as a
 quoted literal rather than read back from anywhere, because every
 caller below builds it from 'gen buffer filler', whose own alphabet is
 letters and digits only - nothing that would need escaping inside the
 literal or inside the failure message.

 'the subject' is read into 'the holder' BEFORE the check, and the
 failure message shows the holder, never the subject directly - hand-
 verified against 0.4.14 that a one-word buffer drawn in its quoted
 spelling ('toolbox', not toolbox - see 'gen buffer name') compiles fine
 as a bare reference (`Print 'toolbox'.`) but not inside a `{...}`
 format slot (`Print "{'toolbox'}".` fails to compile: Unknown variable
 'toolbox'), while neither the bare spelling nor a multi-word quoted
 name ('the inbox') has the problem. 'the holder' comes from 'gen
 buffer companion', which is always multi-word, so it is always safe to
 interpolate; the comparison itself still runs directly against 'the
 subject', which is a plain (unbraced) reference and unaffected. Candidate
 recorded in the batch report - this is the compiler doing something
 surprising, not a rule this leaf gets to adjudicate.

 Two statements, not one: the capture must be its own line so it runs
 before the check reads it, so this returns a two-element list rather
 than the single line 'gen assert true'/'gen assert false' return -
 callers append both elements to their own 'the lines' in order.)

To 'gen assert text is' with a text called 'the row' and a text called 'the subject' and a text called 'the holder' and a text called 'the expected'.
    a text called 'the capture line' is "a text called {'the holder'} is {'the subject'} as text".
    a text called 'the got shown' is 'gen buffer slot of' of 'the holder'.
    a text called 'the line' is "If {'the subject'} is not \"{'the expected'}\" then, Print \"ASSERT {'the row'}: expected {'the expected'} got {'the got shown'}\", Exit 95. Print {'the subject'}".
    a list called 'the pair' is ['the capture line', 'the line'].
    Return a list, 'the pair'.

(BUF-15: `resize`, `reallocate`, `grow`, `shrink` are one operation under
 four names - LANGUAGE.md:3634 lists them with no claim that any one is
 tied to a direction, and hand-verification confirms it: `grow` shrinks
 a buffer just as readily as `shrink` grows one, both taking effect
 exactly like `resize` would. The trailing "bytes" is optional either
 way - LANGUAGE.md's own two examples at the Buffer Resizing heading
 differ only in whether they write it, so that is drawn too.)

