 random overshoot, concatenated once for the append. That keeps 'the
 fitting part' an INDEPENDENT expected value rather than one derived by
 re-truncating the same string the leaf is trying to verify was
 truncated - the same reasoning 'gen leaf buffer resize' below uses for
 BUF-17. Hand-verified against 0.4.14: the append truncates to exactly
 the capacity and raises the error flag, whether the buffer started
 empty or already held a shorter prefix (docs/ledger/probes/buffers/,
 this batch's report); the empty-start case is what is emitted, so
 content = 'the fitting part' exactly.

 The stale-error handler fires first and asserts nothing: On error
 consumes whatever an EARLIER, unrelated statement left pending (the
 lesson 'gen leaf buffer growth' above already paid for - see its own
 comment), so the overflow flag this leaf checks was raised by ITS OWN
 append, not leftover state from three leaves ago.)

