(BUF-36: a dynamic (unsized) DESTINATION buffer grows past whatever small
 initial reserve it started with, whether the growth comes from `append`
 or from `copy`, in a single large write rather than the accumulated
 small-append loop `gen leaf buffer growth` already covers for BUF-03 -
 LANGUAGE.md:3782. Capacity is PRINTED but not asserted: what capacity a
 dynamic buffer actually reports past its initial reserve is exactly
 docs/ledger/buffers.md Discrepancy 1's open question, and encoding this
 leaf's own reading of that dispute as the row's expected answer is the
 false-finding factory CLAUDE.md warns against - the same restraint 'gen
 leaf buffer fullness' already shows for a dynamic buffer's `full`. Only
 `size` (exact, from the payload the generator wrote) and the error flag
 (must stay clear - growth that raised it would itself be the finding)
 are asserted. Top level only: it carries an On error, so it cannot be
 the statement that closes an enclosing block.

 The payload is DIGITS ONLY ('gen random digits', gen_core.vox:78),
 unlike every other leaf here's letters-and-digits 'gen buffer filler' -
 deliberately, not for readability. This is the one leaf in the file
 whose single literal runs to thousands of characters (crossing the
 4096-byte reserve takes that much), and at that length a
 letters-and-digits draw has a real, non-token chance of spelling a
 substring tests/200_never_emitted.vox's guard bans (`halt` is four
 letters over a 36-symbol alphabet - about a 1-in-270 shot per ~6000-byte
 payload, which a 2000-seed sweep of ~600 depth-3 statements each turns
 into odds-on; see this batch's report for the seed 200_never_emitted
 actually caught it at). A pure-digit payload can never contain a
 lowercase-letter needle, so the row's actual claim - size growth - is
 untouched while the coincidence is impossible instead
 of merely unlikely.)

